JWT Authentication Bypass via jku Header Injection
📌 Lab Overview
This lab demonstrates a critical JWT verification vulnerability where the server blindly trusts the jku (JSON Web Key Set URL) parameter supplied inside the JWT header.
Because the application fetches public keys from a user-controlled URL without validating the domain, an attacker can host their own public key, sign arbitrary JWTs using the matching private key, and forge authenticated sessions as any user — including administrator.

🎯 Objective


Forge a valid JWT for the administrator user


Access the /admin panel


Delete the user carlos



🧠 Vulnerability Understanding
🔐 How JWT Verification Normally Works
In RSA-based JWT authentication:


The server signs JWTs using its private key


The server verifies JWTs using its public key


A JWT contains:
HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE
Example payload:
{  "sub": "wiener"}
The signature proves that the payload was signed by the legitimate server.

🧩 What is jku?
jku stands for:
JSON Web Key Set URL
It tells the server where to fetch public keys from.
Example:
{  "alg": "RS256",  "kid": "abc123",  "jku": "https://trusted-site.com/jwks.json"}
The server:


Reads the jku


Downloads the public key set


Uses the specified key (kid) to verify the JWT signature



💥 Root Cause
The application failed to validate whether the jku URL belonged to a trusted domain.
This allowed an attacker to:


Generate their own RSA key pair


Host their own public key


Point jku to the attacker-controlled JWKS


Sign forged JWTs using the matching private key


As a result, the server trusted attacker-generated tokens.

🔥 Attack Flow
Generate RSA Key Pair        ↓Host Attacker Public Key (JWKS)        ↓Modify JWT Payload → sub=administrator        ↓Add malicious jku header        ↓Sign JWT using attacker private key        ↓Server fetches attacker public key        ↓Signature validates successfully        ↓Admin access granted

🛠️ Exploitation Steps
1️⃣ Login as Normal User
Credentials provided by the lab:
wiener : peter
After login, intercept the authenticated request:
GET /my-account
Send the request to Burp Repeater.

2️⃣ Verify Admin Access Restriction
Modify the request path:
GET /admin
The server responds with unauthorized access because the current JWT belongs to wiener.
This confirms that authorization is JWT-based.

3️⃣ Generate RSA Key Pair
Using Burp Suite JWT Editor:
JWT Editor → Keys → New RSA Key → Generate
This creates:


Private key (used for signing)


Public key (used for verification)


Important understanding:
The RSA key pair is independent of the JWT contents.
The same private key can sign any modified payload later.

4️⃣ Create Malicious JWKS
On the exploit server, host a malicious JWK Set:
{  "keys": [    {      "kty": "RSA",      "e": "AQAB",      "kid": "attacker-key",      "n": "..."    }  ]}
The public key was copied directly from Burp:
Copy Public Key as JWK

5️⃣ Modify JWT Header
Original JWT header was modified to include:
{  "alg": "RS256",  "kid": "attacker-key",  "jku": "https://exploit-server-url/exploit"}
Why this works
The vulnerable server blindly trusts the attacker-supplied jku URL and downloads the attacker-controlled public key.

6️⃣ Modify JWT Payload
Payload modified from:
{  "sub": "wiener"}
to:
{  "sub": "administrator"}
This impersonates the administrator account.

7️⃣ Re-sign the JWT
The modified JWT was signed using the attacker-generated private key.
Important concept:
JWT signatures are generated from:
base64(header) + "." + base64(payload)
Therefore, any change to:


header


payload


requires re-signing the token.

8️⃣ Access Admin Panel
The forged JWT was sent in the authenticated request:
GET /admin
The server:


Fetched attacker JWKS from jku


Retrieved attacker public key


Verified signature successfully


Trusted the payload


Result:
Admin panel access granted

9️⃣ Delete User Carlos
The admin panel exposed the following endpoint:
/admin/delete?username=carlos
Sending the request solved the lab.

🔍 Core Security Lessons
❌ Never trust user-controlled key locations
Applications must never blindly trust:


jku


x5u


external JWKS references


without strict validation.

❌ Signature validation is only as secure as key management
Even strong cryptography becomes useless if attackers can control:


which public key gets used


where verification keys are fetched from



✅ Proper Mitigations


Hardcode trusted JWKS domains


Enforce allowlists for jku


Ignore externally supplied key URLs entirely


Pin trusted signing keys


Validate issuer (iss) strictly



🧠 Key Takeaway
This vulnerability was not caused by broken cryptography.
The cryptography worked correctly.
The failure was trusting attacker-controlled key infrastructure during JWT verification.
